SUMMARY:Beware tidy goodness
DESCRIPTION:Flannery O’Connor\n\nMoral self-certainty can become a way of avoiding conversion.\n\nContext: fiction and essays.\n\nO'Connor keeps the comic and the terrible close together. Laughter can expose self-deception more efficiently than solemn moral instruction.\n\nDISTINCTION\nIntegration is not the same as consistency. A self can become more integrated by admitting genuine internal tension.\n\nCONVERSATION\nThree nights ago\, “The self is a landscape” (Macarius of Egypt) sat nearby. Both thoughts treat identity as less settled than it appears. One presses on what the self excludes\; the other on what the self performs\, inherits\, or becomes. Flannery O’Connor's route is grotesque fiction and a Catholic theology of grace\; Macarius of Egypt's is the heart\, interior transformation\, and experiential asceticism.\nEarlier this week\, “Persona is a useful mask” (Carl Jung) sat nearby. Both thoughts treat identity as less settled than it appears. One presses on what the self excludes\; the other on what the self performs\, inherits\, or becomes. Flannery O’Connor's route is grotesque fiction and a Catholic theology of grace\; Carl Jung's is analytic psychology\, symbols\, and individuation.\n\nQUESTION\nWhich character would be most threatened if this idea were actually true rather than merely admirable?\n\nPROVOCATION\nThe person most certain they are the sane one in the room is often the person her fiction has begun to stalk.\n\nCONVERSATION\nRead today's thought backward through Carl Jung: “Persona is a useful mask.” Both refuse the transparent self\; identity contains rooms\, masks\, memories\, habits\, or forces the narrator does not fully command.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
