SUMMARY:Knowing has an asymptote
DESCRIPTION:Nicholas of Cusa\n\nKnowledge can approach its object more exactly while simultaneously clarifying the distance that remains.\n\nContext: On Learned Ignorance.\n\nLearned ignorance is intellectual achievement\, not surrender: the knower becomes more exact about the limits of the knowing instrument.\n\nDISTINCTION\nNot knowing can be either mere absence or a precise recognition that the current instrument has reached its limit.\n\nCONVERSATION\nEarlier this week\, “Loss can simplify love” (John of the Cross) sat nearby. Both thoughts make intellectual limitation productive rather than merely embarrassing. The question is whether the limit invites silence\, better concepts\, or transformed desire. Nicholas of Cusa's route is learned ignorance\, infinity\, and mathematical metaphor\; John of the Cross's is dark night\, detachment\, and transformed desire.\nYesterday\, “Beware tidy goodness” (Flannery O’Connor) sat nearby. Both thoughts weaken the fantasy of a transparent self. What cannot yet be known may include the knower. That makes “Beware tidy goodness” a useful counterpoint to “Knowing has an asymptote.”\n\nDISTINCTION\nApproximation can become indefinitely better without becoming identity.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270828@1am-wisdom-v2
