SUMMARY:Entertainment can refuse
DESCRIPTION:Andy Kaufman\n\nA performer can use the stage to refuse the audience's preferred contract rather than satisfy it.\n\nContext: performance practice.\n\nDISTINCTION\nA failed entertainment and a performance of failed entertainment may be externally identical. The unstable difference is the point.\n\nCONVERSATION\nCarse's open play clarifies something in Kaufman: the strongest performance often leaves the audience without a final\, stable verdict about what game was being played.\n\nHe treats entertainment's social contract as something that can itself be performed\, broken\, or made visible.\n\nQUESTION\nWhat changes if continuation\, rather than victory or payoff\, becomes the criterion of a successful game?\n\nCONVERSATION\nThree nights ago\, “Can the game stay open?” (James P. Carse) sat nearby. Carse's open play clarifies something in Kaufman: the strongest performance often leaves the audience without a final verdict about what game was being played.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
