SUMMARY:The castle has rooms
DESCRIPTION:Teresa of Ávila\n\nDifferent modes of awareness can coexist within one person without all being equally accessible at once.\n\nContext: Interior Castle.\n\nShe is relentlessly empirical about extraordinary experience: intensity proves little unless the experience bears durable fruit in conduct.\n\nQUESTION\nWhich part of the person is being treated as 'the self' here\, and what has been left outside that definition?\n\nCONVERSATION\nThree nights ago\, “Beware tidy goodness” (Flannery O’Connor) sat nearby. Both thoughts treat identity as less settled than it appears. One presses on what the self excludes\; the other on what the self performs\, inherits\, or becomes. Teresa of Ávila's route is interior architecture\, prayer\, and discernment\; Flannery O’Connor's is grotesque fiction and a Catholic theology of grace.\nEarlier this week\, “The self is a landscape” (Macarius of Egypt) sat nearby. Both thoughts treat identity as less settled than it appears. One presses on what the self excludes\; the other on what the self performs\, inherits\, or becomes. Teresa of Ávila's route is interior architecture\, prayer\, and discernment\; Macarius of Egypt's is the heart\, interior transformation\, and experiential asceticism.\n\nIMAGE\nThe self is a castle with rooms inside rooms\; familiarity with the front hall is not knowledge of the whole building.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270830@1am-wisdom-v2
